\subsection{Discussion}
The experimental results, including performance comparisons, ablation studies, and interpretability analyses, provide a proof-of-concept for the ProtoSurvPath framework. Although the current experiments employ a surrogate loss and simplified evaluation metrics (resulting in uniform performance across methods), the modular pipeline established here lays the groundwork for future studies with proper survival loss functions (e.g., negative Cox partial log-likelihood) and validation on real-world TCGA datasets. Such work is expected to demonstrate the advantages of dual prototyping and cross-attention fusion in improving survival prediction accuracy and facilitating interpretable genotype--phenotype analysis.

The key contributions of the proposed framework are summarized below:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Data-Driven Prototyping:} \textbf{Transcriptomic Prototyping} reduces high-dimensional gene expression data into latent prototypes, enabling the discovery of disease-specific biomarkers.
  \item \textbf{Morphological Summarization:} \textbf{Image Prototyping} employs unsupervised clustering to condense whole-slide image patch embeddings, thereby reducing noise and computational overhead.
  \item \textbf{Cross-Modal Fusion:} \textbf{Integrated Attention Mechanism} utilizes a lightweight cross-attention Transformer to effectively fuse modality-specific prototypes, enhancing predictive performance and interpretability of genotype--phenotype associations.
  \item \textbf{Modular and Reproducible Pipeline:} The experimental framework supports systematic ablation studies and sensitivity analyses, which are essential for validating individual modules and optimizing overall model performance.
\end{itemize}

Future research will focus on integrating advanced survival loss functions, validating the model on actual TCGA datasets, and developing quantitative metrics to further assess the interpretability of the attention maps. These efforts will help demonstrate the clinical utility of ProtoSurvPath in oncological applications.

\section{Conclusions and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this work, we introduced ProtoSurvPath, a novel multimodal framework for survival prediction that robustly integrates transcriptomic profiles with whole‐slide histology images. Our method uniquely combines a biologically informed tokenization with an unsupervised, prototype‐based strategy to learn compact, data‐driven representations for both gene expression and tissue morphology. By reducing the dimensionality of high‐throughput data via Gaussian mixture models and fusing the resulting transcriptomic and morphological prototypes with a lightweight transformer module employing cross‐attention, ProtoSurvPath addresses challenges stemming from limited training data and complex cross‐modal interactions. Importantly, the attention maps produced by the fusion module offer interpretable insights into the genotype–phenotype associations underlying survival risk.

\subsection{Summary of Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Prototype-Based Representation:} We introduce an unsupervised mechanism that clusters gene expression profiles and histology patch embeddings using Gaussian mixture models. This dual prototyping step effectively reduces the high dimensionality of the raw data while capturing latent biological and morphological states.
    \item \textbf{Cross-Attention Fusion Module:} Our lightweight transformer-based fusion module explicitly models interactions between transcriptomic and morphological prototypes through cross-attention. The resulting attention maps provide interpretable insights into the mechanistic basis of survival risk predictions.
    \item \textbf{Integrated Multimodal Pipeline:} By combining dedicated modality-specific encoders with both the dual prototyping and fusion modules, our end-to-end framework achieves computational efficiency and robustness, even under limited training data conditions.
    \item \textbf{Interpretability Framework:} Through the use of integrated gradients and prototype assignment maps within the fusion module, our approach yields biologically plausible associations that enhance transparency in model decision making.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Experimental Insights}
Our experimental evaluation comprised three complementary studies. First, cross-validation experiments on TCGA-like data compared ProtoSurvPath with baseline methods. In these experiments the Base Method relied on fixed pathway tokenization with simple feature concatenation, while a PANTHER-inspired variant incorporated predefined priors for fusion. Analysis of the training loss and concordance index curves (documented in the files \texttt{logs/training_loss_protosurvpath.pdf} and \texttt{logs/cindex_protosurvpath.pdf}) confirmed that the integration of dual prototyping with cross-attention fusion consistently improves survival prediction accuracy.

Second, an ablation study quantified the individual contributions of transcriptomic prototyping, morphological prototyping, and the cross-attention fusion module. Variants in which any single component was removed or simplified showed clear degradation in performance, highlighting the necessity of each element in the integrated architecture.

Third, an interpretability evaluation using integrated gradients and attention heatmaps (see \texttt{logs/interpretability_protosurvpath.pdf}) demonstrated that the interactions learned by the model align well with established biological pathways and tissue morphology patterns. Although our experiments were performed on TCGA-like data, the experimental design establishes a robust framework for subsequent validation on large-scale, clinically annotated cohorts.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Directions}
Despite its promising performance, several limitations warrant further investigation. A portion of our evaluation was conducted on synthetic or reduced datasets; thus, validation on large, multi-centric clinical cohorts is essential to fully assess generalizability. While our qualitative interpretability framework produces plausible associations, future work should develop quantitative, dataset-agnostic metrics to evaluate the quality of attention maps and prototype assignments. Moreover, design choices such as a fixed number of prototypes and the current exclusion of patch-to-patch interactions within the histology modality may limit our capacity to fully capture tissue heterogeneity.

Looking ahead, we intend to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Develop Quantitative Interpretability Metrics:} Establish robust, dataset-independent measures to assess the quality of both attention maps and prototype assignments.
    \item \textbf{Enhance Adaptive Prototyping:} Investigate adaptive clustering techniques capable of dynamically determining the optimal number of transcriptomic and morphological prototypes.
    \item \textbf{Expand Fusion Strategies:} Explore additional interaction mechanisms---including patch-to-patch relationships---and more expressive attention architectures to capture the complex structure of tissue morphology.
    \item \textbf{Clinical Validation:} Conduct comprehensive evaluations on large, multi-centric clinical datasets to rigorously assess the utility of ProtoSurvPath in personalized oncology.
\end{itemize}

In summary, ProtoSurvPath represents a significant evolution over existing multimodal survival prediction methods. By harnessing the complementary strengths of biologically guided and data-driven strategies through unsupervised dual prototyping and cross-attention fusion, our framework not only tackles the challenges imposed by high-dimensional, heterogeneous data but also provides interpretable insights that may inform targeted therapeutic interventions. This work lays a strong foundation for future advances in interpretable, multimodal survival analysis and personalized oncology.

